Files Included
=========================
OptimalPathFinder.ipynb 
|-- TertiaryCostMap.py - Map and Obstacle Generator
|-- A.py - Algorithm for Path Optimization
|-- Visualizer.py - Data Visualization
|-- Learn.py - Model Trainer and Statistics
|-- robot_class.py - Robot Movement
||-- South_Clear_Creak - Data Directory
|-- Overlay.ipynp - Visual Aid Generation
|-- plot.tif - Generated Visual Aid

Additional Notes: 
- Utilize a GPU to handle large arrays that will be manipulated using rastor
Methodology
=========================
1)  Converting relevant tif files to maniputable arrays that represent data *Extracting the arrays)
—---------------------------------------------------------------------
Using rasterio package functions to derive arrays of the correct dimensions from given LiDAR data where 0 = Traversable and 1 = Obstacle 
Extract each band within the data into a 2-D array

Create a tertiary cost map
Set all indices in array to traversable by default (0), paths are traversable and unique (2), and obstacles are (1)

2) Generating random coordinates within our graph to become obstacles
—--------------------------------------------------------------------
use rand function and a loop to assign indices to 0

3) Path coordinate values should override any obstacles
—------------------------------------------------------------------

4)  Representing our data functions
—-------------------------------------------------------------------
Derive a graph/visual that represents the path a robot can take - there exist two types and thus two opportunites for 
our data to be represented

Probabilistic Graph: Using percentage/ratio value to represent how likely a point is to be traversable

Binary Graph: Using 0's and 1's as a "Boolean graph"- is the point traversable, yes or no?
classify 0 and 2 as 0
classify 1 as 1
